        "source_handle": "P0261",
        "field_path": [
          "study_summary_A",
          "work_summary"
        ],
        "value": "This review synthesizes current knowledge on how the gut microbiome influences cancer immunotherapy. It begins by summarizing preclinical studies demonstrating that specific commensal bacteria enhance ICI efficacy through T-cell stimulation and metabolite production. It then reviews human observational studies, highlighting the inconsistent identification of predictive microbial signatures across different cohorts and cancer types, while noting the negative impact of broad-spectrum antibiotics. The paper outlines three main mechanistic themes: direct T-cell stimulation, molecular mimicry, and immunomodulatory metabolites (e.g., SCFAs, bile acids, inosine). It also discusses the microbiome's role in immune-related adverse events (irAEs), particularly colitis. Finally, it evaluates emerging therapeutic strategies, including fecal microbiota transplantation (FMT), defined bacterial consortia, probiotics, and dietary interventions, based on results from early-phase clinical trials.",
        "role": "evidence",
        "snapshot_ids": [
          "snapshot::c96dcd391e3ff6d5952be10a",
          "snapshot::eb5aa52fd3ffd49fe0ad1176"
        ]
